  "thumbnailAlt": "Incident Report form beside a photo of a warehouse worker filling in a checklist on a clipboard",
  "content": {
    "intro": "An incident report form is a form that records what happened in a workplace incident, when and where it occurred, who was involved or injured, who witnessed it and what was done in response so a safety team can investigate and prevent it from happening again. This template covers injuries, near misses and property damage in one form, with steps that guide the reporter from the basic facts to causes and corrective actions.",
    "audience": "Health and safety officers, site supervisors, HR teams, warehouse and construction managers, and facility operators in schools, hospitals and retail.",
    "useCases": [
      "Reporting a slip, trip or fall on the shop floor",
      "Logging near misses so hazards are fixed before someone is hurt",
      "Recording vehicle, forklift or equipment damage",
      "Building the records your safety committee and insurer ask for"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Put a QR code on site notice boards and in vehicles so incidents are reported from the scene while details are fresh.",
      "Use email notifications to alert the safety lead the moment a report with an injury comes in.",
      "Use PDF templates to produce a filled incident report for each submission to attach to insurance or investigation files."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should an incident report form include?",
        "answer": "The reporter's details, the date, time and exact location, the type of incident, the people involved, a factual description, any injury and body part affected, first aid or medical treatment given, witnesses, immediate actions taken, likely causes, suggested corrective actions and photos of the scene."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should near misses be reported?",
        "answer": "Yes. A near miss is an event that could have caused injury or damage but did not. Reporting them shows hazards early, and many safety programs treat them as just as important as actual injuries."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I have to report workplace injuries to a regulator?",
        "answer": "Some serious injuries and dangerous occurrences must be reported to a government body, such as OSHA in the US or HSE in the UK, often within a set number of days. The rules vary by country and industry, so check your local requirements; this form is your internal record, not an official filing."
      },
      {
        "question": "How soon should an incident be reported?",
        "answer": "As soon as it is safe to do so, ideally the same shift. Details like times, positions and witness names fade quickly, and early reports make investigations more accurate."
      }
    ]
  },
